    },
    {
      "claim_id": "CC323_EXPECTATION_INTERPRETATION",
      "claim_text": "Capital Chronicle's interpretation is that an expected result need not tell us what fresh information a market will trade on. That is a competing explanation to examine, not proof that anticipation caused these rises.",
      "claim_kind": "FACT",
      "source_id": "SOURCE_2",
      "document_id": "cc323-midterm-source-context-20261106",
      "support_status": "SUPPORTED_EXACT_RETAINED_MAIN_REPORT_SPANS",
      "attribution_required": true,
      "support_excerpt_sha256": "87368e6f34c1cac5917632ff6f8f90e1cad92cefb41e6811adcd994e5333daab"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "CC323_COUNTERCASE",
      "claim_text": "The countercase is not that every part of the dossier failed: the review's gold and dollar directions matched, and a conditional scenario can still organise questions. But one mixed outcome cannot establish a universal mechanism, a forecasting hit rate or the cause of a day's moves.",
      "claim_kind": "FACT",
      "source_id": "SOURCE_1",
      "document_id": "cc323-midterm-lesson-20261106",
      "support_status": "SUPPORTED_EXACT_RETAINED_MAIN_REPORT_SPANS",
      "attribution_required": true,
      "support_excerpt_sha256": "401e5edb86619bb856e5ed6e7f248fb08dbaf70c0ef224fee00895d2b074beb4"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "CC323_FUTURE_LESSON",
      "claim_text": "For a future scenario, Capital Chronicle's lesson is to write down the political condition, the proposed market direction and the horizon separately, then ask what would challenge each. This review shows why the right political answer cannot stand in for that market analysis.",
      "claim_kind": "FACT",
